---
description: Documentation standards for code comments and docstrings
globs: src/**/*.ts, src/**/*.tsx
alwaysApply: false
---

Document why, not what. Only add a comment if it conveys information the code itself cannot. Never narrate what the code obviously does.

Do document: purpose, constraints, trade-offs, non-obvious decisions, known debt (see TODOs below).

Do not document: obvious logic, simple wrappers, self-evident one-liners.

Module/file-level comments: explain what the module owns and any non-obvious limitations.

## Comment kinds

- **`/** */` doc comment** — the contract of an exported symbol, only when its name and types do not already say it: units, edge cases, side effects, failure modes. Write TSDoc style: no types in tags, `@param name - description` with the hyphen, and only tags that add information (`@throws`, `@deprecated` plus what to use instead, `@example`).
- **`//` comment** — why the implementation is the way it is.

## TODOs

`TODO(#123): reason`, pointing at the GitHub issue that tracks it. With no issue, write `TODO: reason` and no link. Never point at a plan or task file, because plans move (see `workflow.mdc`).

## Do not write

- Comments that narrate the change ("now uses X", "added for Y")
- Banner or divider comments
- Comments that restate a name or type
- Edits to comments unrelated to the change
